  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Sat May 30 2026\n\nA band of showers moved through the region earlier this morning\nresulting in beneficial rain for many. Locations along Highway\n275, the Omaha metro, and southwest Iowa saw rainfall totals\nexceeding one inch. The system that resulted in this rainfall\nhas now lifted north of the region allowing for a quiet period\nthis afternoon into the early evening. While it will be muggy,\npartly cloudy skies and temperatures in the low 80s shouldn't\nspoil any Saturday plans.\n\nOur next round of showers and thunderstorms moves into the\nregion afternoon sunset later this evening. Another shortwave\ntrough will lift out of the Rockies later today, this will help\nto initiate thunderstorms along a dryline across central Kansas\ninto the Nebraska Panhandle. This is forecast to develop into an\narcing band of thunderstorms that reaches our area late this\nevening. With instability waning after sunset and weak effective\nshear, these thunderstorms should be on a weakening trend by\nthen. That said, a few strong to severe thunderstorms remain\npossible southwest of a line from Clarinda, to Omaha, and into\nNeligh. Damaging winds and isolated large hail are the main\nthreats with any severe thunderstorms. A wide range of rainfall\ntotals appears likely, with areas that receive stronger \nthunderstorms potentially receiving an additional one to two \ninches. Areas that get missed by the heaviest rainfall may only \nsee a quarter to half inch of rainfall though. Any thunderstorms\nshould begin to dissipate or clear the region shortly after \nmidnight with quieter weather returning by Sunday morning. \n\nMuch of Sunday appears dry with mid-level dry air keeping a cap\non any thunderstorms through much of the day behind the\novernight system. It will remain muggy with temperatures\nclimb into the middle 80s by afternoon. Another round of \nshowers and thunderstorms is possible by late afternoon and \nevening, but chances remain low. Without an additional shortwave\nto help initiate thunderstorms, we will rely on subtle areas of\nconvergence left behind from the previous night. At the moment,\nmuch of the area maintains a low (15-40%) chance of \nthunderstorms through the afternoon and evening. If any \nthunderstorms do develop, there will be enough instability and \nwind shear to support a few strong to severe thunderstorms \ncapable of damaging winds and large hail. \n\nHeading into next week mid-level ridging will begin to take \nshape across the southern and central Plains. This pattern keeps\ntemperatures warm in the 80s and conditions muggy. Afternoon \nand evening showers and thunderstorms will continue to be \npossible, but limited forcing to help initiate these should keep\ncoverage few and far between. That said, most days see a low to\nmedium (15-50%) chance of rain throughout the week. \nUnfortunately, the heat only looks to build by next weekend and \nbeyond.",
